  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">First episode of psychosis with mood features, provisional diagnosis (F29)</content>. Partial response at four weeks: daytime symptoms gone, with residual perceptual disturbances at sleep onset. Intake results support a primary illness rather than a medical cause: urine drug test negative, HIV and syphilis tests nonreactive, TSH 1.8 mIU/L and vitamin B12 388 pg/mL normal. The diagnosis stays provisional. Dr. Feld agrees with a modest dose increase. Safety reviewed: passive thoughts of not waking up less often, no intent or plan; the safety plan and the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline are in his phone; no firearm access. [S]<list><item>Increase aripiprazole to 15 mg once daily.</item><item>Enroll in the coordinated specialty care program: care coordinator, therapist, peer specialist, and help returning to work.</item><item>MRI and EEG results at the next visit.</item><item>Call the team at once for restlessness after the increase.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Alcohol use disorder (F10.20)</content>. Still drinking two or three glasses most evenings. AST 58, ALT 44 and GGT 96 U/L with MCV 102 fL and PEth 284 ng/mL at intake, consistent with heavy drinking; the liver tests are well below three times the upper limit, and he takes no opioids, so naltrexone is suitable. [S]<list><item>Start naltrexone 50 mg once daily.</item><item>Continue thiamine.</item><item>Liver tests and PEth at the six-week visit.</item><item>Carry a card saying he takes naltrexone.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Antipsychotic dose, adherence, and response</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Aripiprazole 10 mg for four weeks with full adherence; partial response; increased to 15 mg</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Psychotic and mood symptoms, and early warning signs</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Name called at sleep onset on some nights; brief night-time suspiciousness; none by day</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Akathisia, parkinsonism, and sedation</td><td>Every visit in the first months and after any dose change</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No akathisia, parkinsonism, or sedation</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Abnormal Involuntary Movement Scale (AIMS)</td><td>Baseline, then every 6 months</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Baseline AIMS score 0, done with the consultation; repeat at six months</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight and BMI</td><td>Every visit for 6 months after starting, then quarterly (CMID: baseline, 4, 8, and 12 weeks, then quarterly)</td><td>Completed today</td><td>64.5 kg at four weeks, from 64.0 at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Waist circumference</td><td>Baseline, then yearly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>86 cm. This baseline was due at intake and was taken today instead; nothing was lost because weight was measured at intake. Waist is now part of the visit template</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Blood pressure</td><td>Baseline, 12 weeks, then yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>128/80 at intake; 124/78 today</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Fasting glucose and A1c</td><td>Baseline, 12 weeks to 4 months, then yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Fasting glucose 91 mg/dL and A1c 5.3% at intake; next at 12 weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Fasting lipid panel</td><td>Baseline, 12 weeks to 4 months, then yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Total cholesterol 184, LDL 106, HDL 52, triglycerides 130 mg/dL at intake; next at 12 weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Prolactin</td><td>If symptoms of high prolactin</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Aripiprazole rarely raises prolactin; no symptoms</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Electrocardiogram</td><td>Before starting if there are cardiac risk factors or QT-prolonging drugs</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No cardiac history, fainting, or QT-prolonging drugs; aripiprazole has little effect on the QT interval</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>First-episode workup (MRI and EEG)</td><td>Once, in the first-episode workup</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Brain MRI and sleep-deprived EEG done this morning; results pending</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression measure (PHQ-9)</td><td>Monthly during acute treatment, every 2 to 4 weeks in continuation, then periodically</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-9 11, from 13; item 9 scored 1</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide-risk review, safety plan, lethal means, and the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Passive thoughts of not waking up less often, no intent or plan; safety plan reviewed; no firearm access; medicines two weeks at a time; the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline in his phone</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Sleep</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>About six hours, broken; regular times and less evening coffee agreed</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Drinking (AUDIT-C and PEth)</td><td>PEth at intake and at each check while drinking continues; AUDIT-C yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>AUDIT-C 9 and PEth 284 ng/mL at intake; two or three glasses most evenings now</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Naltrexone: liver tests and opioid exposure</td><td>Liver tests before starting, then at 1 to 3 months and every 6 to 12 months</td><td>Completed today</td><td>AST 58, ALT 44 U/L at intake, well below three times the upper limit; urine drug test negative for opioids; naltrexone started</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Thiamine</td><td>While drinking heavily</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Taking thiamine 100 mg daily since the hospital</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Psychiatry consultation</td><td>At the start of treatment for a first episode</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Feld today</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Coordinated specialty care engagement</td><td>Ongoing, for up to two to three years</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Enrolled today; first meetings with the care coordinator and therapist booked</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>No return of psychotic symptoms and no readmission through six months, on the lowest effective antipsychotic dose. In progress; target 2026-07-20 [S]</item><item>PHQ-9 score below 5 by six months. In progress; target 2026-07-20 [S]</item><item>Reduce drinking to PEth below 20 ng/mL by six months. In progress; target 2026-07-20 [S]</item><item>Weight gain under 5% and normal glucose, A1c and lipids at the 12-week check. In progress; target 2026-04-20 [S]</item><item>Sleep about seven hours on most nights with regular times by three months. In progress; target 2026-04-20 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: The American Psychiatric Association Practice Guideline for the Treatment of Patients With Schizophrenia, Third Edition (2020); Canadian Network for Mood and Anxiety Treatments (CANMAT) and International Society for Bipolar Disorders (ISBD) 2018 guidelines for the management of patients with bipolar disorder (2018); Consensus development conference on antipsychotic drugs and obesity and diabetes (2004); Recovery After an Initial Schizophrenia Episode (RAISE): coordinated specialty care for first-episode psychosis (2026); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Substance Use Disorders (2021); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Six-week visit in four weeks with liver tests, PEth, the PHQ-9, and the MRI and EEG results. Care coordinator by phone weekly. Call at once for restlessness, voices by day, or dark thoughts. Next scheduled visit 2026-03-02: six-week review: symptoms, side effects, alcohol, sleep, and test results. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
